{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792865102,"nonce":"CEeS1mfK8RJDqxXvanvLqNSefKI=","title":"A heavy function called rarely never reaches LLVM once it is in Cranelift","message":"World.stepSegment (ChimpMonk, CrowdGc scale 1) is called once or twice per World.step, about 800 frames in 15 s, so a thousand or two calls. It takes ~25 ms a call. The LLVM rung is 10000 invocations, so call counting alone never promotes it.\n\nWhat decides it today: an interpreter frame that is inside the function when its Cranelift entry is attached enters it, and the OSR transfer proposes LLVM (\"osr-transfer proposes findex=597 tier=llvm\"). That is a race against the first frame. Six NUC runs, quiet box, ASH_OSR_LOG: 3 of 6 reached LLVM (at 4.2 s, 4.9 s, 8.5 s), 3 never did within 15 s, and the old and new binaries behave alike. When no frame enters, nothing proposes LLVM and the function stays in Cranelift for the whole run (the log shows the Cranelift install at ~0.7 s and no LLVM request after).\n\nCompiled code ticks no back-edges, so a loop-heavy function that is called seldom looks cold. Same family as the \"promotion demand is interpreter-only\" finding.\n\nOptions:\n- Cranelift code ticks the bead at loop back-edges (weighted), so work and not call count drives the rung.\n- Time-weighted promotion from the sampler: promote a function that holds a share of samples.\n- Keep call counts but add a second threshold on accumulated back-edge or sampled time.\n\nMeasure on the NUC with CrowdGc scale 1: first-LLVM-install time of stepSegment over 6+ runs, plus the kernel A/B and bench_tree_fusion.py before landing. Related: 5aaf58e (OSR entry cost), 13f2d56 (ChimpMonk testbed).","files":null}]}